{
  "id": "L-002",
  "title": "Computing Limits",
  "atom_ids": ["A-02"],
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [{ "type": "text", "text": "Computing Limits" }]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Most limits are computed by substitution. The algebraic rules for limits make this legitimate: the limit of a sum is the sum of the limits, and the same holds for products, quotients, and powers — provided the pieces actually have limits and, for a quotient, the denominator does not approach zero."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "equation",
      "source": "\\lim_{x \\to a} \\big(f(x) + g(x)\\big) = \\lim_{x \\to a} f(x) + \\lim_{x \\to a} g(x)",
      "display": true,
      "number": "(2)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So for a polynomial, substitution always works: plug in "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "a" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " and you are done. The interesting cases are quotients where substitution produces "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "0/0" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ". That form is called indeterminate — it tells you nothing yet — and the standard move is to simplify the fraction algebraically and try substitution again."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "worked-example",
      "title": "A limit at a hole",
      "problem": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Compute "
        },
        {
          "type": "math",
          "source": "\\lim_{x \\to -2} \\dfrac{x^2 - 4}{x + 2}"
        },
        { "type": "text", "text": "." }
      ],
      "solution": [
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Substitute "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x = -2" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " to see the form: "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "\\dfrac{4 - 4}{0} = \\dfrac{0}{0}" },
          { "type": "text", "text": ", indeterminate." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Factor the numerator: "
          },
          {
            "type": "math",
            "source": "x^2 - 4 = (x - 2)(x + 2)"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", so for "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x \\neq -2" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " the fraction simplifies to "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x - 2" },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The limit ignores the point "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "x = -2" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " itself, so substituting into the simplified form is valid: "
          },
          {
            "type": "math",
            "source": "\\lim_{x \\to -2} (x - 2) = -4"
          },
          { "type": "text", "text": "." }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "list",
      "ordered": true,
      "items": [
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Substitute first. If you get a real number, that is the limit."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "If you get "
          },
          { "type": "math", "source": "0/0" },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ", simplify the expression — factor, cancel, or combine fractions — and substitute again."
          }
        ],
        [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "If the denominator alone goes to zero, the limit may be infinite or may not exist; investigate the sign on each side."
          }
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "warning",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "0/0" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " form means the limit might be anything — 0, 4, "
        },
        { "type": "math", "source": "-4" },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": ", or undefined. Never report the form itself as an answer."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "widget",
      "name": "plot",
      "version": "1.0",
      "parameters": {
        "series": [
          {
            "label": "f(x) = (x^2 - 4)/(x + 2)",
            "points": [
              [-4.0, -6.0], [-3.5, -5.5], [-3.0, -5.0], [-2.5, -4.5],
              [-2.2, -4.2], [-2.05, -4.05],
              [-1.95, -3.95], [-1.8, -3.8],
              [-1.5, -3.5], [-1.0, -3.0], [-0.5, -2.5], [0.0, -2.0]
            ]
          }
        ],
        "xlabel": "x",
        "ylabel": "y",
        "title": "The hole at x = -2, limit -4"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "formative",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Compute "
        },
        {
          "type": "math",
          "source": "\\lim_{x \\to 4} \\dfrac{x - 4}{\\sqrt{x} - 2}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": " and give the reasoning in one or two sentences."
        }
      ],
      "kind": "free_response"
    }
  ]
}